::GEO_U04_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 9 and 12. What is the hypotenuse? {
=A. 15#Use the Pythagorean Theorem: 9<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> = 225, so c = 15.
~B. 21#Adding the legs gives 21, but side lengths in a right triangle use a sum of squares.
~C. 3#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~D. &radic;21#The legs must be squared before adding.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has hypotenuse 10 and one leg 6. What is the other leg? {
~A. 4#Subtracting 6 from 10 ignores the squared side relationship.
=B. 8#Use b<sup>2</sup> = 10<sup>2</sup> - 6<sup>2</sup> = 64, so b = 8.
~C. 16#Adding 10 and 6 does not find a missing leg.
~D. &radic;136#Adding the squares would find a larger hypotenuse, but the hypotenuse is already known.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ladder is 20 ft long and makes a right triangle with a wall. The base is 12 ft from the wall. How high does the ladder reach? {
~A. 8 ft#Subtracting distances does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~B. 32 ft#This adds the given lengths instead of using squares.
=C. 16 ft#The ladder is the hypotenuse: h<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> = 20<sup>2</sup>, so h = 16 ft.
~D. &radic;544 ft#Adding 20<sup>2</sup> and 12<sup>2</sup> treats both given sides as legs, but 20 is the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>In a right triangle, sin A = <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>. If the hypotenuse is 26, what is the side opposite angle A? {
~A. 5#5 is the ratio numerator, not the scaled side length when the hypotenuse is 26.
~B. 13#13 is part of the ratio, not the opposite side in the larger triangle.
~C. 26#The hypotenuse is 26; the opposite side must match the sine ratio.
=D. 10#Set <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>26</sub> = <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>. Since 26 is twice 13, the opposite side is 10.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has angle A with adjacent side 12 and hypotenuse 13. Which ratio can be used to find angle A? {
=A. cos A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Cosine uses adjacent over hypotenuse, so cos A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>.
~B. sin A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Sine uses opposite over hypotenuse, not adjacent over hypotenuse.
~C. tan A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent and does not use the hypotenuse.
~D. cos A = <sup>13</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#This reverses adjacent over hypotenuse.
}
